@@ -31,3 +31,4 @@ Built for PLAN P3 ("change pipeline with held-out suites") against SPEC v0.12: C
 | C25 | **A release dropped unanswered is offered again (P2-2a f3, L3 on #363).** When `Decided` drops an upstream release proposal because the owner's request closed without the owner's answer (expired, voided, dropped by a restart, a stale approval, a page change), `Lapsed(id)` reports it once, and Loop 3 forgets the version at its next update check and proposes it again under a new ID (`maintain` M8). The owner's NO, an adoption, a refusal and a dispatch failure are not lapses and are never re-offered this way. Nor is a release left out of a partial YES (`why` "not chosen", CH-13): the gate now passes `Decided` the close's reason instead of a declined flag, and such a release is dropped with no decline recorded (arbitrator Q3 on #48) and not reported. CH-13 says only that unanswered items "expire as denied", so whether an omission should be re-asked is not settled. This takes the conservative reading: the owner saw it and chose the others. Like a NO, it is re-asked after a restart (maintain M8). The record is in memory only, like proposals (C9): a restart drops it, and Loop 3 re-asks after a restart anyway. A record for a release Loop 3 no longer asks about (superseded by a newer one) stays until a restart: one short string per lapse. A local change Loop 1 dropped is proposed again only if Loop 1 builds it again. | UPD-5, CHG-3 | Proposals persist (C9): persist the lapse record with them. |
 | C26 | **Attestation authority at proposal (SR3-6).** `ProposeRelease` reads `Verified.Security()` when it proposes, and that now re-checks the box's attestor policy (update U16): a security release checked before the allow-list narrowed, or before the interim rule ended, takes the owner's path rather than CHG-3's standing policy. The adoption's later activation is checked again at `Store.Stage` (apply A9). | UPD-8, CHG-3 | — |
 | C27 | **Settling a staged image adoption (SR3-4).** `ConfirmStaged` and `StageFailed` take the adoption's exact ID, never its short ID, which a revert frees for reuse. Each returns nil when the adoption already settled the same way (`Confirmed`, or reverted) and refuses the other way round or any adoption that was not staged, so the applier can call it again after a cut or an error without confirming or reverting twice. `ConfirmStaged` saves before it reports success and restores the staged state in memory when the save fails. `StageFailed` reverts through the pipeline's journaled revert, so a cut mid-revert finishes the way any revert does. Until it settles, a staged image is reverted for no other reason: the owner's UNDO is refused with "Update N starts at the next restart; undo it after." and the digest offers only MORE, and Recheck's regression and security reverts are refused the same way. An adoption undone while its image may already be installed could never be confirmed, and the applier would wait forever. UNDO is offered once the image has started. | UPD-1, OP-4, OP-5 | — |
+| C28 | **Tree-rule fixtures and finding links (P3-4b-1).** A case whose expect is `ok` (`TreeRuleOK`) and whose input parses as a tree rule is answered by the evaluator from the candidate's tree alone through `AnswerTreeRule` (replay R13), with no machine or model. A fix whose candidate names a finding with no linked case is rejected (`ReasonUnlinked`, fail closed). Loop 2 links both the minimized regression and the original unminimized test to a reported finding (loops S12), so a fix must repair every failing clause. A fix whose candidate names a finding must pass every case linked to that finding (held-back variants included); an unevaluated linked case fails it. Other candidates keep PS1 for those cases (C24). `loopKeys` is a closed forward list: setting a loop-2 or grading key, or leaving that file unreadable, is authority. A `config` fix can still fail when plain probes over config are unevaluable (release finding). | LOOP-10 (a)–(g), CHG-2, A-3 | New loop config keys join `loopKeys` in the package that adds them. |
